WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.989 --> 00:00:05.089
Imagine The Lego Movie and Deadpool got together
and had a PG-rated love child.

00:00:05.089 --> 00:00:07.740
Well, they kind of did: it's The Lego Batman
Movie.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.910
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here!

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:13.839
If you're a Batman fan, then you'll quite
likely feel you've died and gone to Bat-heaven

00:00:13.839 --> 00:00:17.040
when you watch the latest entry in the Lego
movie franchise.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.170
With other fish to fry – hello, Han Solo
movie – Lego Movie directors Phil Lord and

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:25.289
Christopher Miller handed the directing reigns
for this film over to Chris McKay, who edited

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:27.820
and co-directed animation on the first movie.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:32.320
McKay's production is a crazy, colourful,
irreverent ball of energy that races through

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.383
the Caped Crusader's many and varied incarnations
over the years as it spins a tale of dastardly

00:00:35.383 --> 00:00:39.360
deeds by the Joker, while weaving in a story
of family and team spirit that, occasionally,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.310
veers into sentimentality.

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:46.440
Never afraid to poke fun at itself, the Bat-legacy,
movie clichés, or the movie business in general,

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:51.641
the Lego Batman Movie channels the spirit
of 2014's super-successful Lego Movie and

00:00:51.641 --> 00:00:56.160
throws into the mix the kind of barrage of
meta-gags and endless easter eggs so wickedly

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.450
brought to life in Ryan Reynold's Deadpool
movie.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:01.760
In fact, you'll need more than one viewing
to catch them all!

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.379
And, of course, this being a Batman movie,
there's plenty of what you'd expect, including

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:08.229
tricked-out vehicles and costumes, and explosive
action.

00:01:08.229 --> 00:01:13.399
Will Arnett returns as Batman after debuting
his own gravelly-voiced take on the character

00:01:13.399 --> 00:01:14.610
in the first Lego film.

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:19.399
I loved Arnett as Gob in Arrested Development
and Devon in 30 Rock, and he certainly puts

00:01:19.399 --> 00:01:23.280
his comedy chops to good use here, proving
yet again that he was the right choice for

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.900
the part of a preposterously pompous Dark
Knight who's in dire need of having his posturing

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.570
taken down a peg or two.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:33.619
As for the supporting cast, Arnett's Arrested
Development co-star, Michael Cera, gives an

00:01:33.619 --> 00:01:38.060
amusingly enthusiastic performance as Batman
fan-boy and side-kick Robin.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:41.240
While Ralph Fiennes is fantastic as Batman's
butler, Alfred.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.760
Rosario Dawson puts in a nice turn as Barbara
Gordon, jumping across to the world of DC

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.750
from the MCU where she's usually found as
nurse Claire Temple in Marvel's Netflix shows.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:55.270
And as the voice of the Joker, Zach Galifianakis
gives a perfectly serviceable performance,

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:58.549
though I doubt it'll stay with you the way,
say, Mark Hamill's does.

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:02.640
On top of the main players, there's a ton
of cameos including loads of comic-book characters

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.420
as well as a varied bunch of individuals from
the wider world of movies and literature,

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.050
and I'll have a video on that soon as well
as the movie's cool connections and easter

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.050
eggs.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:15.480
Now, if you're thinking, this movie is essentially
one big ad for Lego, well, in many ways, you're

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.530
not wrong, but there's certainly enough fun,
substance, and gorgeous visuals in the film

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.420
to mean it's more than just a platter to serve
up merchandise.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:27.250
Though one thing I did find a bit grating
was the amount of over-the-top product placement

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.050
for a certain smartphone.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.800
If you're looking for a lot of brick-building,
like in the first movie, you won't find it

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.810
here, which makes sense because construction
was a big part of the actual story in the

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.640
Lego Movie whereas here it isn't, and that
didn't bother me at all.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.120
Oh, and you won't find a song either with
the same level of ear-worminess as Everything

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.040
Is Awesome, which, in some ways, is no bad
thing.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.810
Instead, the soundtrack uses 80s pop to often
comical effect and composer Lorne Balfe does

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:57.340
a nice job integrating some rather familiar
superhero themes in his score.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:01.650
To sum up: the Lego Batman Movie does a good
job continuing the entertaining franchise

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.650
kick-started three years ago by Phil Lord
and Chris Miller.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:09.210
It's fun, often funny, silly, and crammed
full of cameos, connections and easter eggs

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:12.910
to the Caped Crusader's world as well as the
DC universe and beyond.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:17.980
Yes, it does sometimes indulge in sentimentality,
much as its predecessor did, but there's much

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.120
to enjoy in Chris McKay's spin-off.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:24.180
And as it's the first superhero movie out
in a packed year of super-flicks, I'm glad

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.920
to say we're off to a good start.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.870
Fingers crossed things carry on that way!

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:30.180
Now, what did you think of the Lego Batman
Movie?

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.480
And who are your favourite DC superheroes
and villains?

